            <ArticleTitle>Adrenocorticotropic hormone infusion as a novel treatment for postdural puncture headache.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>432-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">In two patients, one scheduled for epidural anesthesia and the other for placement of a spinal catheter for operative procedures, severe postdural puncture headache developed and was refractory to conservative therapy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The first patient had several unintentional dural punctures, and the second underwent a planned dural puncture with an 18-gauge needle for insertion of a 20-gauge catheter. When neither patient responded to conservative therapy following development of postdural puncture headache, an infusion of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) was given prior to consideration of epidural blood patching.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Both patients obtained complete and permanent relief from their headaches.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A single treatment with ACTH may offer an alternative therapy in the treatment of postdural puncture headache.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
